---
import '../../styles/global.css';
---
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en" class="scroll-smooth">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8" />
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
  <title>Decoding the Compass Rose: From Ancient Mariners to Digital Navigation</title>
  <meta name="description" content="Discover the complete history, geometry, and modern digital usage of the compass rose in our comprehensive navigation guide." />
  <script is:inline>
    if (typeof localStorage !== 'undefined' && localStorage.getItem('theme')) {
      if (localStorage.getItem('theme') === 'dark') document.documentElement.classList.add('dark');
    } else if (window.matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)').matches) {
      document.documentElement.classList.add('dark');
    }
  </script>
</head>
<body class="bg-zinc-50 dark:bg-[#09090b] text-zinc-900 dark:text-zinc-300 font-sans antialiased transition-colors duration-200 leading-relaxed flex flex-col min-h-screen">
  
  <header class="w-full border-b border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800/80 bg-white/80 dark:bg-[#09090b]/80 backdrop-blur-xl sticky top-0 z-50 transition-colors duration-200">
    <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-3 flex items-center justify-between">
      <a href="/" class="flex items-center gap-3 cursor-pointer">
        <img src="/favicon-96x96.png" alt="Compass Rose Logo" class="w-9 h-9 object-contain drop-shadow-sm" aria-hidden="true" />
        <div class="text-xl font-extrabold tracking-tight text-zinc-900 dark:text-white m-0">Compass Rose</div>
      </a>
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6">
        <nav class="text-sm font-semibold text-zinc-500 dark:text-zinc-400 hidden sm:flex gap-4">
          <a href="/#about" class="hover:text-zinc-900 dark:hover:text-white transition-colors">About Tool</a>
          <a href="/#faq" class="hover:text-zinc-900 dark:hover:text-white transition-colors">FAQ</a>
          <a href="/blog" class="hover:text-zinc-900 dark:hover:text-white transition-colors">Blog</a>
        </nav>
        
        <button id="theme-toggle" class="p-2.5 rounded-xl bg-zinc-100 dark:bg-zinc-800/50 text-zinc-500 dark:text-zinc-400 hover:bg-zinc-200 dark:hover:bg-zinc-800 transition-colors border border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-700/50 focus:outline-none" aria-label="Toggle Dark Mode">
          <svg id="theme-toggle-dark-icon" class="hidden w-5 h-5" fill="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 20 20">
            <path d="M17.293 13.293A8 8 0 016.707 2.707a8.001 8.001 0 1010.586 10.586z"></path>
          </svg>
          <svg id="theme-toggle-light-icon" class="hidden w-5 h-5" fill="currentColor" viewBox="0 0 20 20">
            <path d="M10 2a1 1 0 011 1v1a1 1 0 11-2 0V3a1 1 0 011-1zm4 8a4 4 0 11-8 0 4 4 0 018 0zm-.464 4.95l.707.707a1 1 0 001.414-1.414l-.707-.707a1 1 0 00-1.414 1.414zm2.12-10.607a1 1 0 010 1.414l-.706.707a1 1 0 11-1.414-1.414l.707-.707a1 1 0 011.414 0zM17 11a1 1 0 100-2h-1a1 1 0 100 2h1zm-7 4a1 1 0 011 1v1a1 1 0 11-2 0v-1a1 1 0 011-1zM5.05 6.464A1 1 0 106.465 5.05l-.708-.707a1 1 0 00-1.414 1.414l.707.707zm1.414 8.486l-.707.707a1 1 0 01-1.414-1.414l.707-.707a1 1 0 011.414 1.414zM4 11a1 1 0 100-2H3a1 1 0 000 2h1z" fill-rule="evenodd" clip-rule="evenodd"></path>
          </svg>
        </button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </header>

  <article class="max-w-4xl w-full mx-auto px-4 py-12 sm:py-20 flex-grow">
    
    <header class="mb-12 border-b border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800 pb-8">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mb-6">
        <span class="px-3 py-1 rounded-full bg-rose-500/10 text-rose-600 dark:text-rose-400 text-sm font-bold uppercase tracking-wider">History & Design</span>
        <span class="text-sm text-zinc-500">10 Min Read</span>
      </div>
      <h1 class="text-4xl sm:text-5xl lg:text-6xl font-black tracking-tight text-zinc-900 dark:text-white leading-tight mb-8">Decoding the Compass Rose: From Ancient Mariners to Digital Navigation</h1>
      
      <!-- Top Hero Image -->
      <div class="w-full aspect-[21/9] bg-zinc-200 dark:bg-zinc-800 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-xl border border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800">
        <img src="/blog-hero.jpg" alt="Timeline of a compass transitioning from parchment to digital" class="w-full h-full object-cover" onerror="this.src='https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1524661135-423995f22d0b?auto=format&fit=crop&q=80&w=2000'" />
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="space-y-8 text-lg text-zinc-700 dark:text-zinc-300 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
      
      <p>For centuries, a single geometric star has stood between humanity and being hopelessly lost. Whether you are examining a dust-covered parchment map from the 15th century or relying on a high-speed digital compass on your smartphone, the compass rose remains the beating heart of all navigation.</p>
      
      <p>But this emblem is far more than just a cluster of arrows pointing North. It is a brilliant intersection of ancient meteorology, human culture, mathematical design, and planetary science. Let's dive deep into the anatomy of the compass rose, tracing its growth from ancient wind maps to the digital sensors in your pocket.</p>

      <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-zinc-900 dark:text-white mt-16 mb-6">1. The History: From Wind Catchers to Magnetism</h2>
      
      <p>The origins of the compass rose have nothing to do with magnetism. Early Mediterranean mariners navigated by the sky and the wind. The earliest "roses" were actually wind roses—charts that tracked the direction of the eight principal winds of the Mediterranean Sea.</p>
      
      <p>Before the magnetic needle was introduced to Europe, sailors memorized winds like the icy Tramontana from the North and the warm Scirocco from the Southeast. When cartographers began drawing these directional winds on maps, they used intersecting lines radiating from a central point. As these lines crossed, they created a star-like, petaled pattern—which is precisely why it was dubbed a "rose."</p>
      
      <p>When the magnetized needle arrived from ancient China and was paired with these wind charts in the 1300s, the modern compass rose was born. It fused the physical reality of Earth's magnetism with the established visual language of the wind rose.</p>

      <!-- Sticky Scroll Timeline Animation -->
      <div id="timeline-container" class="relative h-[250vh] w-full my-16">
        <div class="sticky top-24 w-full aspect-[4/3] sm:aspect-video rounded-2xl overflow-hidden shadow-2xl border border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800 bg-[#09090b]">
          
          <!-- Image 1: Ancient Greek -->
          <img id="scroll-img-1" src="/timeline-1.jpg" alt="Ancient Greek Wind Chart" class="absolute inset-0 w-full h-full object-cover transition-all duration-700 ease-in-out scale-100 opacity-100 z-30" />
          
          <!-- Image 2: Medieval -->
          <img id="scroll-img-2" src="/timeline-2.jpg" alt="Medieval Compass Rose" class="absolute inset-0 w-full h-full object-cover transition-all duration-700 ease-in-out scale-105 opacity-0 z-20" />
          
          <!-- Image 3: Digital -->
          <img id="scroll-img-3" src="/timeline-3.jpg" alt="Modern Digital Compass" class="absolute inset-0 w-full h-full object-cover transition-all duration-700 ease-in-out scale-105 opacity-0 z-10" />
          
          <!-- Overlay Text -->
          <div class="absolute bottom-6 left-0 w-full text-center z-40 px-4">
            <span id="scroll-text" class="inline-block px-4 py-2 rounded-full bg-zinc-900/80 backdrop-blur text-white text-sm font-bold tracking-wider shadow-lg transition-all duration-500">
              c. 300 BCE: The Wind Rose
            </span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-zinc-900 dark:text-white mt-16 mb-6">2. The Masterclass in Design & Geometry</h2>
      
      <!-- Side-by-side Layout for Text and Image -->
      <div class="flex flex-col md:flex-row gap-8 items-start mb-8">
        <div class="flex-1 space-y-6">
          <p>The design of the compass rose is a fascinating study in functional UX (User Experience) design, created hundreds of years before the concept of UX existed. Every shape, color, and line was engineered for life-or-death readability under terrible conditions—such as reading a map by a flickering candle on a pitching ship.</p>
          
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-zinc-900 dark:text-white">The Hidden Symbolism in the Shapes</h3>
          <p>If you look at a classic cartographic compass rose, you will notice that North and East are almost always uniquely decorated:</p>
          <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 marker:text-rose-500">
            <li><strong>The Fleur-de-lis (North):</strong> Cartographers traditionally marked the North point with an ornate lily or spearhead. This made it instantly recognizable, allowing a navigator to orient the map in a fraction of a second.</li>
            <li><strong>The Cross (East):</strong> On medieval European maps, the East point was often marked with a Christian cross. This was because Jerusalem lay to the East of Europe, and early maps were oriented with East at the top.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        
        <!-- Secondary Image (Design) -->
        <div class="w-full md:w-1/3 shrink-0 bg-zinc-200 dark:bg-zinc-800 rounded-xl overflow-hidden shadow-lg border border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800">
          <img src="/blog-design.jpg" alt="Vintage map compass rose with Fleur-de-lis" class="w-full h-auto object-cover aspect-[4/5]" onerror="this.src='https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1549423985-79b88231c69c?auto=format&fit=crop&q=80&w=800'" />
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-zinc-900 dark:text-white mt-8 mb-3">The Strategic Color Palette</h3>
      <p>Why do classic compass roses look so vibrant? It wasn't just for decoration. Early mapmakers used a strict color-coding system to prevent sailors from misreading the intersecting lines:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 marker:text-rose-500">
        <li><strong>Black:</strong> Used exclusively for the 8 principal winds/cardinal directions to provide maximum contrast.</li>
        <li><strong>Green or Blue:</strong> Used for the half-winds (intercardinal points).</li>
        <li><strong>Red:</strong> Used for the quarter-winds, ensuring they didn't visually blend into the primary black lines.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-zinc-900 dark:text-white mt-16 mb-6">3. How to Read a 32-Point Compass Rose</h2>
      
      <p>To truly understand maritime navigation, you need to know how to read the full 32-point compass rose. Traditional navigation relied on breaking the circle into 32 equal slices, each measuring exactly 11.25 degrees.</p>
      
      <p>Hover over or tap the table rows below to see how the geometry builds upon itself on the compass dial.</p>

      <!-- Interactive Grid: Table + SVG -->
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-2 gap-8 items-center bg-zinc-100 dark:bg-zinc-900/50 p-6 rounded-2xl border border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800 my-8">
        
        <!-- Left: Interactive Table -->
        <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-zinc-300 dark:border-zinc-700 w-full shadow-sm">
          <table class="w-full text-left border-collapse text-sm bg-white dark:bg-[#09090b]">
            <thead>
              <tr class="bg-zinc-200 dark:bg-zinc-800 text-zinc-900 dark:text-white">
                <th class="p-3 font-bold uppercase tracking-wider border-b border-zinc-300 dark:border-zinc-700">Tier</th>
                <th class="p-3 font-bold uppercase tracking-wider border-b border-zinc-300 dark:border-zinc-700">Degrees</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="divide-y divide-zinc-200 dark:divide-zinc-800">
              <tr class="interactive-row cursor-pointer transition-colors hover:bg-rose-50 dark:hover:bg-rose-500/10" data-target=".point-primary">
                <td class="p-3 font-semibold text-rose-500">Primary (Cardinal)</td>
                <td class="p-3 font-mono text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-400">0°, 90°, 180°, 270°</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="interactive-row cursor-pointer transition-colors hover:bg-rose-50 dark:hover:bg-rose-500/10" data-target=".point-secondary">
                <td class="p-3 font-semibold text-rose-500">Secondary (Intercardinal)</td>
                <td class="p-3 font-mono text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-400">45°, 135°, 225°, 315°</td>
              </tr>
              <tr class="interactive-row cursor-pointer transition-colors hover:bg-rose-50 dark:hover:bg-rose-500/10" data-target=".point-tertiary">
                <td class="p-3 font-semibold text-rose-500">Tertiary (Half-winds)</td>
                <td class="p-3 font-mono text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-400">22.5°, 67.5°, 112.5°...</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <!-- Right: Interactive SVG Compass -->
        <div class="flex justify-center items-center w-full bg-[#121214] p-8 rounded-xl border border-zinc-800 shadow-inner">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 400 400" class="w-64 h-64 select-none pointer-events-none drop-shadow-[0_0_15px_rgba(255,255,255,0.05)]">
            <circle cx="200" cy="200" r="190" fill="none" stroke="#27272a" stroke-width="2" />
            <circle cx="200" cy="200" r="150" fill="none" stroke="#27272a" stroke-width="1" stroke-dasharray="4,4" />
            
            <g transform="rotate(45 200 200)">
              <!-- Tertiary Points -->
              <polygon points="200,200 200,65 186,186" class="compass-point point-tertiary transition-colors duration-300" fill="#3f3f46" />
              <polygon points="200,200 200,65 214,186" class="compass-point point-tertiary transition-colors duration-300" fill="#52525b" />
              <polygon points="200,200 335,200 214,186" class="compass-point point-tertiary transition-colors duration-300" fill="#3f3f46" />
              <polygon points="200,200 335,200 214,214" class="compass-point point-tertiary transition-colors duration-300" fill="#52525b" />
              <polygon points="200,200 200,335 214,214" class="compass-point point-tertiary transition-colors duration-300" fill="#3f3f46" />
              <polygon points="200,200 200,335 186,214" class="compass-point point-tertiary transition-colors duration-300" fill="#52525b" />
              <polygon points="200,200 65,200 186,214" class="compass-point point-tertiary transition-colors duration-300" fill="#3f3f46" />
              <polygon points="200,200 65,200 186,186" class="compass-point point-tertiary transition-colors duration-300" fill="#52525b" />
              
              <!-- Secondary Points -->
              <polygon points="200,200 200,42 178,178" class="compass-point point-secondary transition-colors duration-300" fill="#71717a" />
              <polygon points="200,200 200,42 222,178" class="compass-point point-secondary transition-colors duration-300" fill="#52525b" />
              <polygon points="200,200 358,200 222,178" class="compass-point point-secondary transition-colors duration-300" fill="#71717a" />
              <polygon points="200,200 358,200 222,222" class="compass-point point-secondary transition-colors duration-300" fill="#52525b" />
              <polygon points="200,200 200,358 222,222" class="compass-point point-secondary transition-colors duration-300" fill="#71717a" />
              <polygon points="200,200 200,358 178,222" class="compass-point point-secondary transition-colors duration-300" fill="#52525b" />
              <polygon points="200,200 42,200 178,222" class="compass-point point-secondary transition-colors duration-300" fill="#71717a" />
              <polygon points="200,200 42,200 178,178" class="compass-point point-secondary transition-colors duration-300" fill="#52525b" />
            </g>

            <!-- Primary Points (North, East, South, West) -->
            <polygon points="200,200 200,20 174,174" class="compass-point point-primary transition-colors duration-300" fill="#d4d4d8" />
            <polygon points="200,200 200,20 226,174" class="compass-point point-primary transition-colors duration-300" fill="#a1a1aa" />
            <polygon points="200,200 380,200 226,174" class="compass-point point-primary transition-colors duration-300" fill="#d4d4d8" />
            <polygon points="200,200 380,200 226,226" class="compass-point point-primary transition-colors duration-300" fill="#a1a1aa" />
            <polygon points="200,200 200,380 226,226" class="compass-point point-primary transition-colors duration-300" fill="#d4d4d8" />
            <polygon points="200,200 200,380 174,226" class="compass-point point-primary transition-colors duration-300" fill="#a1a1aa" />
            <polygon points="200,200 20,200 174,226" class="compass-point point-primary transition-colors duration-300" fill="#d4d4d8" />
            <polygon points="200,200 20,200 174,174" class="compass-point point-primary transition-colors duration-300" fill="#a1a1aa" />

            <circle cx="200" cy="200" r="14" fill="#18181b" stroke="#52525b" stroke-width="2" />
          </svg>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-zinc-900 dark:text-white mt-16 mb-6">4. The Science: Working With the Magnetic Compass</h2>
      
      <!-- Side-by-side Layout for Text and Image -->
      <div class="flex flex-col md:flex-row-reverse gap-8 items-start mb-8">
        <div class="flex-1 space-y-6">
          <p>Deep beneath the Earth's crust, churning liquid iron creates a massive geodynamo. This generates a magnetic field that envelops the globe. A traditional compass contains a lightweight, magnetized needle balanced on a nearly frictionless pivot. Because the Earth's magnetic North Pole emits an attractive force, the "North" end of the needle is constantly pulled into alignment with these invisible magnetic field lines.</p>
          <p>However, working with a magnetic compass requires accounting for <strong>Magnetic Declination</strong>. The Earth's geographic North Pole (True North) and the magnetic North Pole are not in the exact same place. Depending on where you are standing on the planet, your compass might point several degrees away from True North. Navigators must mathematically adjust their compass rose readings to correct this error.</p>
        </div>
        
        <!-- Tertiary Image (Science) -->
        <div class="w-full md:w-1/3 shrink-0 bg-zinc-200 dark:bg-zinc-800 rounded-xl overflow-hidden shadow-lg border border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800">
          <img src="/blog-science.jpg" alt="Magnetic field lines crossing a smartphone" class="w-full h-auto object-cover aspect-[4/5]" onerror="this.src='https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1579309403816-c85ee581b212?auto=format&fit=crop&q=80&w=800'" />
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold text-zinc-900 dark:text-white mt-16 mb-6">5. Modern Usage: The Compass Rose in the Digital Age</h2>
      
      <p>Today, you don't need a heavy brass instrument to find your way. The modern usage of the compass rose lives inside web browsers and smartphone hardware.</p>
      
      <p>Modern mobile devices are equipped with micro-electromechanical systems (MEMS)—specifically, a magnetometer to read the Earth's magnetic field, and an accelerometer to understand how the phone is tilted. When combined through software, these sensors generate a highly accurate, digital compass rose on your screen.</p>
      
      <div class="my-10 p-8 bg-gradient-to-br from-rose-50 to-white dark:from-rose-500/10 dark:to-zinc-900/50 rounded-2xl border border-rose-200 dark:border-rose-900/30 shadow-lg text-center">
        <h3 class="text-2xl font-black text-zinc-900 dark:text-white mb-4">Ready to test your device's sensors?</h3>
        <p class="mb-6 text-zinc-700 dark:text-zinc-300">Experience this centuries-old geometry powered by cutting-edge web technology. Find True North, track real-time degrees, and measure surface tilt—all directly from your browser.</p>
        <a href="/" class="inline-block px-8 py-3 bg-rose-500 hover:bg-rose-600 text-white font-bold rounded-full transition-colors shadow-md">
          Launch Digital Compass Tool
        </a>
      </div>

      <p class="text-center font-medium italic text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-400">The medium has changed from parchment to pixels, but the compass rose remains exactly what it has always been: our ultimate guide through the unknown.</p>
    </div>
  </article>

  <footer class="w-full bg-zinc-50 dark:bg-[#09090b] py-10 border-t border-zinc-200 dark:border-zinc-800 transition-colors duration-200 mt-auto">
    <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 flex flex-col items-center gap-6">
      <nav class="flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-x-8 gap-y-3 text-sm font-semibold text-zinc-500 dark:text-zinc-400">
        <a href="/about" class="hover:text-zinc-900 dark:hover:text-white transition-colors">About Us</a>
        <a href="/privacy" class="hover:text-zinc-900 dark:hover:text-white transition-colors">Privacy Policy</a>
        <a href="/terms" class="hover:text-zinc-900 dark:hover:text-white transition-colors">Terms & Conditions</a>
        <a href="/contact" class="hover:text-zinc-900 dark:hover:text-white transition-colors">Contact Us</a>
      </nav>
      <p class="text-sm font-semibold text-zinc-400 dark:text-zinc-600">&copy; 2026 Compass Rose Tool. Free to use, no account needed.</p>
    </div>
  </footer>

  <script>
    // Theme Toggle Logic
    const themeToggleBtn = document.getElementById('theme-toggle');
    const darkIcon = document.getElementById('theme-toggle-dark-icon');
    const lightIcon = document.getElementById('theme-toggle-light-icon');

    if (document.documentElement.classList.contains('dark')) {
      lightIcon?.classList.remove('hidden');
    } else {
      darkIcon?.classList.remove('hidden');
    }

    themeToggleBtn?.addEventListener('click', () => {
      document.documentElement.classList.toggle('dark');
      if (document.documentElement.classList.contains('dark')) {
        localStorage.setItem('theme', 'dark');
        lightIcon?.classList.remove('hidden');
        darkIcon?.classList.add('hidden');
      } else {
        localStorage.setItem('theme', 'light');
        darkIcon?.classList.remove('hidden');
        lightIcon?.classList.add('hidden');
      }
    });

    // Interactive SVG Compass Logic
    const rows = document.querySelectorAll('.interactive-row');
    const allPoints = document.querySelectorAll('.compass-point');

    rows.forEach(row => {
      // Mouse Enter / Touch Start
      const triggerHighlight = () => {
        const targetClass = row.getAttribute('data-target');
        
        // Dim all points
        allPoints.forEach(p => {
          // Store original fill colors as data attributes if not already done
          if (!p.hasAttribute('data-original-fill')) {
            p.setAttribute('data-original-fill', p.getAttribute('fill'));
          }
          p.setAttribute('fill', '#27272a'); // Dimmed dark gray
        });

        // Highlight targeted tier with Rose Red
        document.querySelectorAll(targetClass).forEach(p => {
          p.setAttribute('fill', '#f43f5e'); // Tailwind rose-500
        });
      };

      // Mouse Leave / Touch End
      const resetHighlight = () => {
        allPoints.forEach(p => {
          // Restore original colors
          const original = p.getAttribute('data-original-fill');
          if (original) p.setAttribute('fill', original);
        });
      };

      row.addEventListener('mouseenter', triggerHighlight);
      row.addEventListener('mouseleave', resetHighlight);
      row.addEventListener('touchstart', (e) => { e.preventDefault(); triggerHighlight(); });
      row.addEventListener('touchend', resetHighlight);
    });

    // Sticky Scroll Timeline Logic
    const timelineContainer = document.getElementById('timeline-container');
    const img1 = document.getElementById('scroll-img-1');
    const img2 = document.getElementById('scroll-img-2');
    const img3 = document.getElementById('scroll-img-3');
    const scrollText = document.getElementById('scroll-text');

    if (timelineContainer) {
      window.addEventListener('scroll', () => {
        const rect = timelineContainer.getBoundingClientRect();
        
        // Calculate scroll progress through the container (0 to 1)
        const scrollMax = rect.height - window.innerHeight;
        let progress = -rect.top / scrollMax;
        
        // Clamp progress between 0 and 1
        progress = Math.max(0, Math.min(1, progress));

        // State 1: Ancient
        if (progress < 0.33) {
          img1.classList.replace('opacity-0', 'opacity-100');
          img1.classList.replace('scale-105', 'scale-100');
          
          img2.classList.replace('opacity-100', 'opacity-0');
          img2.classList.replace('scale-100', 'scale-105');
          
          scrollText.textContent = "c. 300 BCE: The Wind Rose";
        } 
        // State 2: Medieval
        else if (progress >= 0.33 && progress < 0.66) {
          img1.classList.replace('opacity-100', 'opacity-0');
          img1.classList.replace('scale-100', 'scale-105');
          
          img2.classList.replace('opacity-0', 'opacity-100');
          img2.classList.replace('scale-105', 'scale-100');
          
          img3.classList.replace('opacity-100', 'opacity-0');
          img3.classList.replace('scale-100', 'scale-105');
          
          scrollText.textContent = "c. 1300 CE: The Magnetic Map Rose";
        } 
        // State 3: Digital
        else {
          img2.classList.replace('opacity-100', 'opacity-0');
          img2.classList.replace('scale-100', 'scale-105');
          
          img3.classList.replace('opacity-0', 'opacity-100');
          img3.classList.replace('scale-105', 'scale-100');
          
          scrollText.textContent = "Today: The Digital Sensor Dial";
        }
      }, { passive: true });
    }
  </script>
</body>
</html>